  "content": {
    "id": "p36-q5",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "The pH of a liquid is traditionally defined on a logarithmic scale as $\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log_{10}(x)$, where $x$ is the hydrogen ion concentration in moles per litre (mol/L). The greater the concentration of hydrogen ions, the more acidic it is.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p36-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Rewrite the formula with $x$ as the subject.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=10^{-\\mathrm{pH}}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\log_{10}(x)&=-\\mathrm{pH}\\\\x&=10^{-\\mathrm{pH}}\\end{align*}$$\nHere $x>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 8
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Pure water has a pH of about 7. What is its hydrogen ion concentration?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Approximately $10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{mol/L}$.",
          "worked": "$$x\\approx10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{mol/L}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 8
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p36-q5-c-prompt",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Lemon juice typically has a pH of about 2.0. What is its hydrogen ion concentration?",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "break"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "How many times more acidic is it than pure water?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Approximately $0.01\\ \\mathrm{mol/L}$; approximately $100\\,000$ times as acidic, meaning $100\\,000$ times the hydrogen ion concentration.",
          "worked": "Using the nominal pH values:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=10^{-2.0}\\\\x&=0.01\\ \\mathrm{mol/L}\\\\\\frac{x_{\\mathrm{lemon}}}{x_{\\mathrm{water}}}&=\\frac{0.01}{1\\times10^{-7}}\\\\\\frac{x_{\\mathrm{lemon}}}{x_{\\mathrm{water}}}&=100\\,000\\end{align*}$$\nThe concentration is approximately $0.01\\ \\mathrm{mol/L}$, approximately $100\\,000$ times that of pure water.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-q5-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p36-q5-d-prompt",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Sea water typically has a pH of about 8. What is its hydrogen ion concentration?",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "break"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "How does this concentration compare with pure water?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Approximately $10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{mol/L}$; approximately one tenth the hydrogen ion concentration of pure water.",
          "worked": "Using the nominal pH values:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{mol/L}\\\\\\frac{x_{\\mathrm{sea}}}{x_{\\mathrm{water}}}&=\\frac{10^{-8}}{10^{-7}}\\\\\\frac{x_{\\mathrm{sea}}}{x_{\\mathrm{water}}}&=\\frac{1}{10}\\end{align*}$$\nSea water has approximately one tenth the hydrogen ion concentration of pure water.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      }
    ]
  },
